<Page title="Permissions" description="Why the extension declares only activeTab, scripting, and storage, and which capabilities it deliberately excludes.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Public Product Contract / Permissions</p><h1>Three permissions.<br />One explicit page.</h1><p class="lead">The Manifest V3 permission budget is exactly <code>activeTab</code>, <code>scripting</code>, and <code>storage</code>. Incognito is <code>not_allowed</code>.</p>
  <dl><dt><strong><code>activeTab</code></strong></dt><dd>Temporary access associated with your toolbar action on the current tab. Product authorization is narrower than Chrome's technical grant: Page Exit and observed same-document navigation end it and require another explicit toolbar action. No persistent host access is requested.</dd><dt><strong><code>scripting</code></strong></dt><dd>Injects the Engine and detachable UI into the authorized top-level HTML Document, in the ISOLATED world only. No child-frame injection, MAIN-world execution, History monkey patch, or page communication bridge is used.</dd><dt><strong><code>storage</code></strong></dt><dd>Retains one validated reduced result per tab in trusted-context-only session storage. Active source values, DOM references, and the Mutation Journal stay in memory. No permanent or synchronized result history is kept.</dd></dl>
  <h2>Absent permissions and capabilities</h2><p>No host permissions, persistent content-script match patterns, <code>tabs</code>, <code>webNavigation</code>, <code>clipboardWrite</code>, <code>downloads</code>, <code>debugger</code>, <code>cookies</code>, <code>history</code>, <code>webRequest</code>, or network interception permissions are declared.</p><p>Limited tab lifecycle and identity APIs are used without the broad <code>tabs</code> permission. Download Report uses a transient local Blob URL and the browser’s native .md download handling during your click, with selectable Markdown if initiation fails. It requests neither <code>downloads</code> nor <code>clipboardWrite</code> and does not write to the clipboard. The extension has no application network client, telemetry, remote code, screenshot capture, or automatic export.</p>
  <h2>Permission does not make every page supported</h2><p>Chrome-protected pages, non-HTML documents, direct files, other unsupported protocols, iframe documents, and Shadow DOM remain outside the Supported Scope. The extension does not request more access to bypass these restrictions.</p><p><a href="/scope/">Read the Supported Scope</a> and <a href="/privacy/">data lifecycle</a> before authorizing a page.</p>
</Page>
